ciasis causes ElephanUasls Blindness Cirrhosis Brain oedema Leopard, Lizard, nr elephant skin like lesion occurs in Onchocerciasis Leishmanimsis"},{"label":"D","text":"Chugas disease Punctate keratitls 1s neen In Schistosomlasis Filariasis Onchocerclasls Cysticereosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-03abf6a1f088","stem":"Cellular Pathology I.• f?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-32df19170796","stem":"When the infarction is 3-5days old, the necrosis and inflammation are most extensive,and the myocardium is the softest, so that transmural infarction can be complicated by rupture. A papillary muscle may rupture as well to produce a sudden valvular insufficiency. Rupture through the septum produce a left-right shunt and right heart failure.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Towards the end of the week, healing of the infarction becomes more apparent with capillaries, fibroblasts and macrophages become filled with hemosiderin. The granulation tissue seen here is more prominent from 2-3weeks following onset of infarction."},{"label":"B","text":"After a couple of weeks, healing is well under way, and there is extensive collagen deposition."},{"label":"C","text":"The myocytes here are hypertrophied marked by the large dark nuclei, and there is interstitial fibrosis. This is an example of cardiomyopathy. In this case, long-standing occlusive atherosclerosis led to ‘ischemic’ cardiomyopathy."},{"label":"D","text":"This is an acute MI of the anterior left ventricular free wall and septum in cross section. Note that the infarction is nearly transmural. There is a yellowish center with necrosis and inflammation surrounded by a hyperemic border."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ad848f339086","stem":"Ideal leaders according to a survey are commonly characterized as all of the following except A. Honest B. Competent C. Forward-looking D. Cooperative9. According to a survey research in terms of leadership experiences, A. Women tended to describe themselves in transactional terms B. Men tended to describe themselves in somewhat transformational terms C. Men influenced others primarily in terms of personal characteristics D. Women helped subordinates develop commitment for broader goals than their own self-interest10. What is commonly thought of as participative management?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a660c11a2586","stem":"Women leaders’ interactive leadership style is developed A. Due to their socialization experiences and career paths B. Because of exchanges with subordinates for services rendered C. By influencing others primarily through their organizational position and authority D. Because of their organizational position12. According to female executives, what is the major factor that prevents senior female executives from advancing to corporate leadership? A. Lack of significant general management B. Male stereotyping and preconceptions C. Inhospitable corporate culture D. Exclusion from informal networks13. According to female executives. which of the following factors least prevents women from advancing to corporate leadership? A. Women not being in the pipeline long enough B. Exclusion from informal networks C. Inhospitable corporate culture D. Lack of significant line experience","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-36efc447cf62","stem":"What is seen as an investment that can help change counterproductive behavior, often interpersonal, that threatens to derail an otherwise valued manager? A. Mentoring B. Executive coaching C. Team building D. Change management Top of Form 26222220 Modern managers are: a. action oriented. b. able to build a sense of shared values. c. able to manage change efficiently. d. all of the above. 21 Planning, organizing, directing and controlling are the: a. functions of management. b. goals of management. c. results of management. d. all of the above. 22 Coordinating people and human resources to accomplish organizational goals is the process of: +9a. planning. b. directing. c. management. d. leadership. 23 Which of the following would be included in the \"controlling function\" ? a. measuring results against corporate objectives. b. explaining routines. c. setting standards. d. giving assignments. 24 Specific, short-term statements detailing how to achieve an organization's goals is known as its: a. vision. b. mission statement. c. objectives. d. operational planning. 25 An outline of the fundamental purpose of an organization is called its: a. Mission statement. b. Objectives. c. Policy. d. a&b","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-35e398535982","stem":"23 Which of the following would be included in the \"controlling function\" ?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9dcefa52c6c1","stem":"LaToya is comparing a number of health insurance plans to offer her employees. Which management function is she MAINLY working on?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4c52c52be565","stem":"Supervisors are MAINLY concerned with which management function?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-83ba9c4e2dc0","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a good way to develop leadership skills?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-073c001123e3","stem":"Circumscribed, developmental skin defect A. Nevus B. Macule C. Papule D. Pustule24. Inflammation secondary to rupture of a hair follicle A. Furunculosis B. Pustule C. Vesicle D. Plaque25. What is a bulla?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c9f8e506809f","stem":"Which AB is responsible for atopy?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bd23cdd36022ec133cb3c28b97b2a8a88ab8f009"},{"id":"q-8f72c5478ba4","stem":"Where are scabies mites found?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8d437db997c29d259d4f4e79c0a44d813b29bac6"},{"id":"q-f7b57407eb15","stem":"Age of onsent for puppy pyoderma A. 2-9 Months B. 3-6 months C. 6 months- 1 year D. 8 weeks-6 months33. What is the transition phase of a hair follicle?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-718c85193172","stem":"In this condition, there is a scaly dermatitis affecting parts of the skin that are supplied by oil glands. A. chronic dermatitis B. contact dermatitis C. eczema D. seborrheic dermatitis35. Which of the following is a fungal infection?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Dermatology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Dermatology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-48a0aa8636a9","stem":"Yellowing of the skin is indicative of A. hyperbillirubinemia B. hyperuricemia C. hyperkalemia D. hyporeninemia40. A chronic dermatitis of unknown etiology in patients with a history of allergy is called A. actinic dermatitis B. atopic dermatitis C. stasis dermatitis D. seborrheic dermatitis41. The brown-black pigment of the skin that is transferred to other epidermal cells and gives the skin its color is called A. albumin B. collagen C. keratin D. melanin42. Which of the following infections is also known as ringworm? A. folliculitis B. herpes simplex C. impetigo D. tinea corporis43. Another term for itching is A. dermatitis B. keratosis C. petechiae D. pruritus44.A 65-year-old man suffered a massive myocardial infarction that was complicated by shock and prolonged hypotension. On arrival in the emergency department he was found to have focal neurological signs in addition to features consistent with low-output cardiac failure. Despite the best efforts of the medical team he died the next day. At autopsy, the most likely change you would expect to see in a brain biopsy would be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute haemorrhagic change"},{"label":"B","text":"Coagulative necrosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Granulomatous change"},{"label":"D","text":"Lacunar infarct"},{"label":"E","text":"Liquefactive necrosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82bca22294bc","stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a chronic infective lesion on her leg underwent a full-thickness examination of this lesion a rim of multinuclear giant cells is seen. The central region is most likely to show","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Caseous necrosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophilic necrosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Fibrinous necrosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Foam cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Pyogenic necrosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5b40c678ec11","stem":"A skin biopsy from an anorexic 16-year-old girlshowed cellular atrophy. During atrophy","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The cell disappears"},{"label":"B","text":"Cellular organelles swell"},{"label":"C","text":"Cell size decreases"},{"label":"D","text":"Cell size increases"},{"label":"E","text":"Protein synthesis increases"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aa7ad7de02bd","stem":"A 35-year-old man is a habitual smoker. If a biopsy is taken from the respiratory tract in this man, the epithelium of respiratory tract is most likelyto show","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mucous hyperplasia"},{"label":"B","text":"Smooth-muscle hyperplasia"},{"label":"C","text":"Squamous cell anaplasia"},{"label":"D","text":"Squamous cell hypertrophy"},{"label":"E","text":"Stratified squamous metaplasia"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a7368c1d62fd","stem":"Hypertrophy can be physiological or pathological and is caused by increased functional demand or by specific hormonal stimulation. Hypertrophy can be best described as","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abnormal deposition in a cell"},{"label":"B","text":"Change in cell morphology"},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease in cell size"},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in cell number"},{"label":"E","text":"Increase in cell size and in its organelles"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bba7a5264d53","stem":"Apoptotic cells usually exhibit distinctive morphological features. Which one of the following morphological features is usually seen in pure apoptosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cellular swelling"},{"label":"B","text":"Chromatin condensation"},{"label":"C","text":"Early disruption of the plasma membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"Nuclear stabilisation"},{"label":"E","text":"Phagocytosis of apoptotic bodies by neutrophils"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9e964237c36","stem":"A histopathologist reports fat necrosis after examining a slide. Fat necrosis might be found in which one of the following situations?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Brain injury"},{"label":"B","text":"Muscle injury"},{"label":"C","text":"Trauma to the abdomen"},{"label":"D","text":"Trauma to the breast"},{"label":"E","text":"Trauma to the bowel"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-469484d99be6","stem":"A healthy 26-year-old man fractured his right tibia in a road traffic accident. His right leg was immobilised in a plaster cast. The castwas removed from his leg after 8 weeks of immobilisation. Which of the followingchanges is most likely to have taken place in his gastrocnemius muscle after this time?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decrease in the number of muscle fibres"},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease in the number of nerve fibres"},{"label":"C","text":"Increase in the number of fast fibres"},{"label":"D","text":"Increase in the mitochondrial content"},{"label":"E","text":"Increase in the number of satellite cells"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a0604a60154c","stem":"A 21-year-old man sustained a severe soft-tissue injury following a road traffic accident. Which ofthe following metabolic effects is most likely to follow this injury?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased aldosterone secretion"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibition of gluconeogenesis"},{"label":"C","text":"Mobilisation of fat stores"},{"label":"D","text":"Protein anabolism"},{"label":"E","text":"Respiratory alkalosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fc0bf893b62e","stem":".A 32-year-old man, working in a power plant, was exposed to radioactive material. He is most likely to suffer radiation injury due to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Decreased intracellular Na+"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreased intracellular Ca2+"},{"label":"C","text":"Free radical formation"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased adenosine triphosphate (ATP) production"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of protein synthesis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2476ed8b6114","stem":". A 62-year-old diabetic and hypertensive man suffered a stroke which affected his speech and movement in the right arm and leg. A cerebral angiogram revealed an occlusion of his left middle cerebral artery. Months later, a computed tomographic (CT) scan shows a large, 5-cm cystic area in his left parietal lobe cortex. This CT finding most likely demonstrates a lesion that is the consequence of resolution of which of the following events?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Apoptosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Atrophy"},{"label":"C","text":"Caseous necrosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Coagulative necrosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Liquefactive necrosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-099e68091f94","stem":"A 25-year-old woman breastfed her first baby for almost 1 year with no difficulties and no complications. Which of the following cellular processes that occurred in the breast during pregnancy allowed her to nurse the infant for this period of time?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ductal epithelial metaplasia"},{"label":"B","text":"Epithelial dysplasia"},{"label":"C","text":"Lobular hyperplasia"},{"label":"D","text":"Stromal hypertrophy"},{"label":"E","text":"Steatocyte atrophy"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1067f7cfba74","stem":".An 80-year-old woman was found dead in her room in a nursing home one morning. An autopsy was carried out and her death was reported as being secondary to old age. At autopsy, her heart was small (250 g) and dark-brown in colour on section. Microscopically, there was light-brown perinuclear pigment seen after haematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining of the cardiac muscle fibres. Which of the following substances is most likely to be increased in the myocardial fibres to produce this cardiac appearance?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Calcium, following necrosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Cholesterol, as a consequence of atherosclerosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Glycogen, resulting from a storage disease"},{"label":"D","text":"Haemosiderin, resulting from iron overload"},{"label":"E","text":"Lipochrome, from ‘wear and tear’"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b6e69fa061eb","stem":"A 73-year-old woman with long-standing hypertension and aortic stenosis died suddenly one morning. An autopsy was performed on her body. At autopsy, her heart weighed 540 g. The size of her heart is most likely to be the result of which of the following processes involving the myocardial fibres?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fatty degeneration"},{"label":"B","text":"Fatty infiltration"},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperplasia"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypertrophy"},{"label":"E","text":"Oedema"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-26850c9d162b","stem":". Metaplasia is a reversible change in which one adult cell type (epithelial or mesenchymal) is replaced by another adult cell type. In which of the following situations is the process of epithelial metaplasia most likely to have occurred?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute myocardial infarction"},{"label":"B","text":"Lactation following pregnancy"},{"label":"C","text":"Tanning of the skin following sunlight exposure"},{"label":"D","text":"Urinary obstruction due to an enlarged prostate"},{"label":"E","text":"Vitamin A deficiency"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b5ae626d7913","stem":". A 55-year-old woman with chronic atrial fibrillation suddenly developed an acute abdomen and was rushed to the emergency department. At emergency laparoscopy most of the small-bowel loops were dusky to purple-red in colour. Her mesenteric veins were patent. The most probable underlying pathological process is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Emergency Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coagulative necrosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Dry gangrene"},{"label":"C","text":"Gas gangrene"},{"label":"D","text":"Liquefactive gangrene"},{"label":"E","text":"Wet gangrene"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Emergency Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fdf6d68c5b89","stem":"Which of the following changes is associated with irreversible cell damage?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"glycogen depletion"},{"label":"B","text":"flocculent densities in mitochondria"},{"label":"C","text":"cellular swelling."},{"label":"D","text":"loss of microvilli."},{"label":"E","text":"detachment of ribosomes from rER."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c13caa53dbb01aaa9138669bddfd3fccb313edab"},{"id":"q-78e015032ade","stem":".Irreversible cellular injury is characterised by all, EXCEPT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"progressive loss of phospholipids."},{"label":"B","text":"decreased activity of Na+ / K+ ATPase causing cellular swelling."},{"label":"C","text":"damage to the cellular cytoskeleton."},{"label":"D","text":"the presence of reactive O2 species."},{"label":"E","text":"the loss of the intracellular amino acids glycine and L-alanine."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"400afb052465c6336108802d244891881fb7f885"},{"id":"q-8de711b5e2d3","stem":"Free radicals","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"are not a byproduct of metabolism."},{"label":"B","text":"can only form in the presence of oxygen."},{"label":"C","text":"may arise by absorption of radiant energy."},{"label":"D","text":"are removed with the aid of Vitamin A derivatives."},{"label":"E","text":"contain an extra protein that may bind to and destroy organic molecules."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-13f3fe79447c","stem":".Apoptosis occurs in all but one of the following situations","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"embryogenesis."},{"label":"B","text":"bacterial infection."},{"label":"C","text":"menstruation."},{"label":"D","text":"tumours."},{"label":"E","text":"viral infection."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ddf94a33194727a3a86c1686ed7de450d9b5515b"},{"id":"q-0d48d182dcd5","stem":".Apoptosis results in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"recruitment of neutrophils."},{"label":"B","text":"swelling and lysis of cells."},{"label":"C","text":"phagocytosis of apoptotic cells by surrounding healthy cells."},{"label":"D","text":"release of free radicals."},{"label":"E","text":"localised oedema."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"30d21fe6748f83cf633471612012329fb2d7847b"},{"id":"q-e63513d405c0","stem":".A substance that is not known to accumulate intracellularly is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"melanin."},{"label":"B","text":"haemosiderin."},{"label":"C","text":"tattoo ink."},{"label":"D","text":"pus."},{"label":"E","text":"lipofuscin."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"4a7f11f94081b4d8df5f2cac96cc76c7157ae95b"},{"id":"q-e727f591a786","stem":"Concerning vascular leakage with acute inflammation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Pathology","topic":"General and Systemic Pathology","topicOriginal":"General and Systemic Pathology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"endothelial cell contraction preferentially occurs in arterioles 20-60 μm in diameter."},{"label":"B","text":"tumour necrosis factor (TNF) affects leakage by direct endothelial cell necrosis."},{"label":"C","text":"immediate sustained response is due to widening of intercellular gaps."},{"label":"D","text":"chemically mediated endothelial contraction is usually reversible."},{"label":"E","text":"transcytosis is not thought to be a potential mechanism of increased permeability."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Pathology","displayTopic":"General and Systemic Pathology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemG