Empyema"},{"label":"D","text":"Pneumothorax"},{"label":"E","text":"Pyothorax"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4a95a07c3f5a","stem":"A 53-year-old man develops weakness, malaise, cough with bloody sputum, and night sweats. A chest x-ray reveals numerous apical densities bilaterally, some of which are cavitary. Exposure to Mycobacterium tuberculosis was documented 20 years ago, and M. tuberculosis is identified in his sputum. Which of the following describes the expected lung pathology in this patient?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Respiratory Medicine","topicOriginal":"Respiratory Medicine","subtopic":"Tuberculosis","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dense fibrosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Eosinophilic infiltration"},{"label":"C","text":"Granulomas"},{"label":"D","text":"Interstitial pneumonia"},{"label":"E","text":"Plasma cell infiltration"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Respiratory Medicine","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9f336bce2e5b","stem":"A 22-year-old man with AIDS complains of persistent cough, night sweats, low-grade fever, and general malaise. A chest x-ray reveals an area of condolidation in the periphery of the left upper lobe, as well as hilar lymphadenopathy. Sputum cultures show acid-fast bacilli. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bronchopneumonia"},{"label":"B","text":"Pulmonary abscess"},{"label":"C","text":"Sarcoidosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Tuberculosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Wegener granulomatosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-a782dc721b91","stem":"A 60-year-old alcoholic woman presents to the emergency room with fever, chills, and shortness of breath. The sputum is rusty-yellow and contains numerous neutrophils, red blood cells, and gran-positive cocci. A chest x-ray shows diffuse haziness over both lungs. One week following admission, the patient develops empyema. This pulmonary condition is associated with the spread of bacterial infection to which of the following anatomic locations?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchi"},{"label":"C","text":"Interstitial space"},{"label":"D","text":"Pericardium"},{"label":"E","text":"Pleural space"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","a