Group":"aa433305db7edb9aea207030cbf09cada3f50c9f"},{"id":"q-7414d33a99e5","stem":"Which of the following is a fourth generation cephalosporin?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"348d2af69b027325f5356c0577942060c4ff47b5"},{"id":"q-dc006b66313f","stem":"Which of the following statements about imipenem is most accurate?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"70158aaeaa8f5abd0bc7076e5fd9a22e4c57872e"},{"id":"q-62373f8a5d57","stem":"Which of the following statements correctly pairs the antifungal drugs with their likely mechanism of action?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphotericin B - inhibits thymidylate synthetase"},{"label":"B","text":"Griseofulvin - interferes with microtubule function"},{"label":"C","text":"Flucytosine - inhibits fungal mycolic acid synthesis"},{"label":"D","text":"Miconazole - inhibits DNA dependent RNA polymerase"},{"label":"E","text":"Nystatin - inhibits ergosterol synthesis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-af7c6b7131cc","stem":"Which of the following statements INCORRECTLY pairs the antifungal drug with its typical adverse effect?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphotericin B - normocytic anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Griseofulvin - photosensitivity"},{"label":"C","text":"Flucytosine - myelosuppression"},{"label":"D","text":"Ketoconazole - menstrual irregularities"},{"label":"E","text":"Nystatin - mental confusion"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-eca6eb41868c","stem":"Which of the following statements best explains the mechanism of antifungal action of amphotericin B?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of topoisomerase II"},{"label":"B","text":"Impairment of the normal permeability of fungal cell membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of reverse transcriptase"},{"label":"D","text":"Blockade of fungal mitosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of fungal cytochrome P450 enzymes"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7240c363293c","stem":"Which of the following microorganisms is sensitive to amphotericin B ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coccidioides immitis"},{"label":"B","text":"Mycoplasma pneumoniae"},{"label":"C","text":"Chlamydia psittaci"},{"label":"D","text":"Nocardia asteroides"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8227239950fe","stem":"Which of the following statements regarding the toxicity of amphotericin B is correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metabolic alkalosis is frequent and may be pronounced"},{"label":"B","text":"A shock-like fall in blood pressure may occur during IV infusion"},{"label":"C","text":"A sudden decrease of body temperature may occur during IV infusion"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd4369f88a1e","stem":"Which of the following is an antifungal drug that acts by inhibiting fungal DNA synthesis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluorouracil"},{"label":"B","text":"Cytarabine"},{"label":"C","text":"Flucytosine"},{"label":"D","text":"Griseofulvin"},{"label":"E","text":"Ketoconazole"},{"label":"F","text":"Terbinafine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f795ece21fb0562a4c562920f7f42be80628324e"},{"id":"q-9ae717d728b3","stem":"Which of the following statements best explains the mechanism of antifungal action of azoles?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of conversion of squalene to lanosterol"},{"label":"B","text":"Formation of artificial pores in the fungal membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of fungal mitosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of squalene synthesis"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-62c4cc6767d8","stem":"Which of the following fungal diseases is resistant to azole treatment?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Coccidioidomycosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Histoplasmosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Aspergillosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Cryptococcosis"},{"label":"E","text":"Mucormycosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bad20eff70ad","stem":"Which of the following fungi are sensitive to Griseofulvin?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Candida albicans"},{"label":"B","text":"Malassezia furfur"},{"label":"C","text":"Trichophyton rubrum"},{"label":"D","text":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},{"label":"E","text":"Histoplasma capsulatum"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0c658f8e0192","stem":"Recognized clinical indications for the use of terbinafine include which of the following diseases?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actinomycosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Brucellosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxoplasmosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Onychomycosis Answers"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dba846344456ed1be11ce0e382c5b4ed69ed858d"},{"id":"q-1c0a44a38b31","stem":"Recognized clinical indications for the use of terbinafine include which of the following diseases?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Actinomycosis"},{"label":"B","text":"Brucellosis"},{"label":"C","text":"Toxoplasmosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Onychomycosis"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dba846344456ed1be11ce0e382c5b4ed69ed858d"},{"id":"q-1f4b9b585be3","stem":"Griseofulvin is effective only against dermatophytes which take up the drug via an active transport system. Its exact mechanism of action is still unclear but the drug appears to disrupt the mitotic spindle trough interaction with microtubules.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of thymidylate synthetase would be the mechanism of action of flucytosine."},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibition of mycolic acid synthesis would be the mechanism of action of isoniazid."},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibition of DNA dependent RNA polymerase would be the mechanism of action of rifampin"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis would be the mechanism of action of azoles"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-80ed112028a0","stem":"Nystatin is given only topically or by oral route for intestinal candidiasis. In both cases the drug is not absorbed so central adverse effects are quite unlikely.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amphotericin B causes nephrotoxicity that can affect up to 80% of patients The decreased production of erythropoietin frequently leads to a normocytic anemia."},{"label":"B","text":"Griseofulvin is an allergenic drugthat can cause many hypersensitivity reactions includi ng photosensitivity."},{"label":"C","text":"Flucytosine may be partially converted to 5-fluorouracil by the intestinal flora of the host and so can exhibit some characteristic effects of anticancer drugs like myelosuppression (up to 13% of patients) and alopecia."},{"label":"D","text":"Ketocona zole can affect gonadal hormone synthesis by inhibiting the Cytochrome P450 system. This can cause menstrual irregularities in women and impotence in men."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8dd5a8cbf0d1","stem":"Amphotericin B binds to fungal cell membrane and causes the formation of artificial pores through which ions and small molecules are lost. This causes the death of the fungal cell.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of quinolones."},{"label":"C","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of several antiviral drugs."},{"label":"D","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of griseofulvin."},{"label":"E","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of azoles."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2c6acd055c2","stem":"Amphotericin B is a toxic drug and more than 50% of patients treated with this drug undergo some adverse effects. These can be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute, infusion-related: IV infusion can cause headache, arthralgias, nausea and vomiting, fever and chills, hyperpnea, and a shock-like fall in blood pressure (concomitant administration of antipyretics, antihistamines, glucocorticoids and meperidine have all been shown to reduce these effects)"},{"label":"B","text":"Chronic, organ-related: a dose-limiting nephrotoxicity, with decreased GFR, azotemia, renal tubular acidosis, and renal wasting of K+ and Mg++ can affect up to 80% of patients. The decreased production of eryt hropoietin frequently causes a normocytic anemia. Amphotericin B toxicity may be reduced by the use of lipid formulations, but the improvement of clinical efficacy is moderated, at best."},{"label":"A","text":"Acidosis, not alkalosis, usually occurs"},{"label":"C","text":"Fever, not hypothermia, can actually occur during IV infusion"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe37a255efd2","stem":"Which of the following is an antifungal drug that acts byinhibiting fungal DNA synthesis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fluorouracil"},{"label":"B","text":"Cytarabine"},{"label":"C","text":"Flucytosine"},{"label":"D","text":"Griseofulvin"},{"label":"E","text":"Ketoconazole"},{"label":"F","text":"Terbinafine"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f795ece21fb0562a4c562920f7f42be80628324e"},{"id":"q-c6c0fd5a926f","stem":"Azoles inhibit cytochrome P450 enzymes. This inhibition impairs the activity of a P450-dependent lanosterol demethylase that converts lanosterol to ergosterol. The ultimate effect is an inhibition of ergosterol synthesis in fungal cell s. The specificity of azole drugs results from their greater affinity for fungal than for mammalian cytochrome P450 enzymes. Imidazoles (i.e ketoconazole) have a lesser degree of specificity than triazoles (i.e. fluconazole).","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Proteins and Enzymes","topicOriginal":"Proteins and Enzymes","subtopic":"Enzyme","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This would be the mechanismof action of terbinafine."},{"label":"B","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of amphotericin B"},{"label":"C","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of griseofulvin."},{"label":"D","text":"No antifungal drug can inhibit the synthesis of squalene."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Proteins and Enzymes","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-effd3959a28e","stem":"List Paragraph;Quote;Intense Quote","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ee87ca731818","stem":"Intravenous Verapamil","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"May terminate supra ventricular tachycardia. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Must not be given to patients receiving beta blockers. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces Digoxin excretion. T"},{"label":"D","text":"One must delay DC-cardio version at least 2 hours after a dose. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Shortens the PR-interval. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b2be618938eec53b9f8a5c63e9b90664c72f6ebc"},{"id":"q-208348725757","stem":"Nifedipine in comparison to Verapamil is more likely to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Worsen angina. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Cause ankle swelling unresponsive to diuretics. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Have a negative inotropic effects. F"},{"label":"D","text":"Cause flushing and headache. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Cause constipation. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"19c09b47128faec7c8c69f64a9ccb1848b3b632d"},{"id":"q-4924355b2845","stem":"Insulin causes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction of hepatic output of glucose T."},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction of protein synthesis. F"},{"label":"C","text":"Enhanced transit of potassium into cells. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased glucose uptake in peripheral tissues. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Down regulation of insulin receptors when its concentration is high: a factor in the insulin resistance of obese diabetics. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"da78919f35f45898cbd29370b85a5476d035d5c2"},{"id":"q-30ec4d0e8883","stem":"The following statement about therapy with iron is true","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parenteral administration produces a more rapid rise in haemoglobin than oral administration. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Desferrioxamine is useful in the treatment over dosage with iron preparation T"},{"label":"C","text":"Ascorbic acid impairs the efficiency of iron absorption F (Phenytoin, Phenobabiton, Primidon)"},{"label":"D","text":"Iron preparation should not be taken at same time as with tea which contains tannate. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Gastro-intestinal contains side effects which limit the use of oral iron preparations. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8f7e2ea421ed545e89fa47cd58a0da6f4a638d5d"},{"id":"q-8ddca06527c5","stem":"Folic acid","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alone provides adequate treatment for some cases of pernicious anemia F"},{"label":"B","text":"Is absorbed in the large intestine. F"},{"label":"C","text":"Is only active when converted to tetrahydrofolic acid. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Is not present in green vegetables other than spinach F"},{"label":"E","text":"Should be used routely in pregnancy in a dose of about 300ug a day. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d56ce3177877","stem":"Recombinant human erythropoietin is used to treat","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron deficient anemia when iron malabsorbed. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Pernicious anemia. F"},{"label":"C","text":"Anemia at chronic renal failure. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Zidovudine-induced anemia. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Clozapine-induced agranulocytosis. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"31b2146342ad903ae011d655decf9c99fa17452d"},{"id":"q-3a0655e47096","stem":"Filgrastim (human granulocyte colony stimulating factor)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is usually administered by subcutaneous injection. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Causes immediate transient neutropenia. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Is used in myeloid leukaemia. F"},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulated proliferation and differentiation of progenitor cell of all granulocyte cell. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Causes bone pain. T Options for question 16-21. s"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d083e1205591f51de54743020de1d21319232e06"},{"id":"q-8e73051b6aa0","stem":"Drugs used to treat hormone dependent cancer","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This drug is selective estrogen receptor modulator ( SERM ) 26 | Tamoxifen"},{"label":"B","text":"Is an androgen receptor antagonist 11 | Flutamide"},{"label":"C","text":"Is an aromatize inhibitor 1 | Aminoglutethimide"},{"label":"D","text":"Is a GnRH agonist 18 | Leuprolide"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a alpha reductase inhibitor 10 | Finasteride"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8f5d11d01d29e1ff17558291aa5ea36dcfd6dbfc"},{"id":"q-c08c958a9dc0","stem":"Drugs used to treat infertility","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is used to treat male and female infertility. 12 | Gonadorelin"},{"label":"B","text":"Is a SERM. 5 | Clomifene"},{"label":"C","text":"Is a dopamine agonist. 2 | Bromocriptine"},{"label":"D","text":"Is FSH from postmenopausal urine. 29 | Urofillitropin"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a LH agonist. 13 | hCG"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bf02c172c1fdb26c87a0c2aceb93b015afa55c51"},{"id":"q-29dd20b195e5","stem":"These drugs have the following properties","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is used to treat female hirsutism. 6 | Cyproterone"},{"label":"B","text":"Is not orally active due to inactivation by 1st pass metabolism. 27 | Testosterone"},{"label":"C","text":"Is in a formulation that provides a pulsatile pattern of drug delivery. 12 | Gonadorelin"},{"label":"D","text":"Is available as a yearly implant. 8 | Ethinyloestradiol"},{"label":"E","text":"Is not orally active because it is a peptide. 24 | Oxytocin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"db1c4f8e3552b720a0dcb9795248aed634b90fa8"},{"id":"q-fecce13ba8d2","stem":"Drugs used during labor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a prostaglandin (PGE1) analogue for inducing therapeutic abortion. 22 | Misoprostol"},{"label":"B","text":"Is a prostaglandin (PGF2-alpha) used to control postpartum hemorrhage. 4 | Carboprost"},{"label":"C","text":"Is a peptide hormone used to induce labour. 24 | Oxytocin"},{"label":"D","text":"Is a progesterone receptor antagonists and pretreatment for therapeutic abortion. 21 | Mifepristone"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a non steroidal anti inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to inhibit labor. 15 | Indomethacin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f4294a5166b0dd4d454de155849c021ff67aa266"},{"id":"q-2b87b4521067","stem":"These drugs produce the following side effects","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Endometrial cancer. 26 | Tamoxifen"},{"label":"B","text":"Ovarian hyper stimulation syndrome. 29 | Urofillitropin"},{"label":"C","text":"Gall bladder disease. 8 | Ethinyloestradiol"},{"label":"D","text":"Acne, weight gain, hirsutism (virilization). 23 | Norethndrone"},{"label":"E","text":"Asthma. 4 | Carboprost"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b1cd8a1c5293eeab38bbe2c45bca98509f82321f"},{"id":"q-44d175c960e6","stem":"Antithyroid drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a thioamine preferred for fragments and inhibits 5-deiodinase. 25 | Propylthiouracil"},{"label":"B","text":"Concentrates in the thyroid destroying this gland. 14 | 131 I"},{"label":"C","text":"Has transient efficacy but is useful pretreatment for thyroid surgery. 16 | Iodine"},{"label":"D","text":"This radiopaque material has limited use due to the risk of aplastic anemia. 28 | Thiocyanate"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a prodrug of methimazole. 3 | Carbimazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"44ab3c09c8cffdda3f1582d5bbfa92083bd098f1"},{"id":"q-86bb81ad47f6","stem":"Corticosteroids when used in ulcerative colitis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibit immune response of B-cells and macrophages. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Require dose reduction as symptoms improve. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Are used in long term as it is in chrohn’s conditions. F"},{"label":"D","text":"May precipitate hyperglycemia in diabetic patients. T"},{"label":"E","text":"May make an individual susceptible to infections. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"79e2c5ec85b3fd466b63e48efe43a6d3973449a3"},{"id":"q-7c8d8c8ffc1a","stem":"Codaine phosphase","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has antiemetic, antitusive, analgesic, antidiarrhea effect. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Act on H and K receptors.T"},{"label":"C","text":"In diarrhea it cause decrease in its motility and reduces secretion. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Is not effective in severe diarrhea. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Is an opiod derivative. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9597020bbf6d7c93de1cdff072f2cb98427a62b3"},{"id":"q-d841c160af96","stem":"Sumatriptan | Antimigraine","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a 5-HT3 agonist F"},{"label":"B","text":"Is not as potent as Ergometrine in reducing migraine symptoms.F"},{"label":"C","text":"Is associated with many side effects F"},{"label":"D","text":"Is contraindicated in ischaemic heart diseases.T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a potent vasodilator. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-375b662743b7","stem":"Heparin | Parenteral Anticoagulant","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cannot pass though water filled pores within thee gastro intestinal mucosa. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Antagonizes the action of Antithrombin III. F | Binds to Antithrombin III"},{"label":"C","text":"Causes osteomalacia when rised in long term therapy. F | Osteoporosis & Hemorrhage"},{"label":"D","text":"Is excreted in the urine entirely unchanged. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Is only admitted parenterally for systemic effects T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ca072a3f3b85","stem":"Warfarin | Oral Anticoagulant","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits synthesis of vitamin K dependent cloting factors. T | Prothrombin, Factor VII, IX & X, Anticoag' Prot C & S"},{"label":"B","text":"Produces therapeutic effects within 30-60 minutes after asministration. F – FEW HOURS"},{"label":"C","text":"Is administed intravernously. F ORAL"},{"label":"D","text":"Is highly bound to plasma protein. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Commonly causes echymosis at site of venous puncture. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4281278378f9","stem":"Streptokinase | fibrinolytic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is an enzyme derived from streptococci and is useful in dissolving fibrin. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Antistreptococcal antibodies may inhibits fibrinolytic activity. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Produces fibrin and fibrinogen degradation products which enhance its activity. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Has distributed half life which is in a different from its biological half life. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Produces allergic reactions which can be prevented by pre-treatment with hydrocortisone T."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-450abdb4f79b","stem":"Urokinase | fibrinolytic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is an enzyme that can be extracted from human urine. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Is less antigenic than streptokinase. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Has systemic lytic effects which are more marked than those of streptokinase. F"},{"label":"D","text":"Is less expensive than streptokinase. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Has half-life of about 2 hours. T 146 MINUTES"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-65b21dccb17e","stem":"Tranexamic acid | Antifibrinolytic | oral/IV","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits the action of plasmin. F PLASMINOGEN Activation"},{"label":"B","text":"Is orally active.T"},{"label":"C","text":"Should not be given to patients who are at risk of bleeding. F | shud be 4 Rx"},{"label":"D","text":"Is useful in treatment of upper gastrointestinal hemorrhage. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibits the action of pepsin.T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5f5d21a480fb","stem":"Loop diuretics","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Diuretic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cause metabolic alkalosis. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase the excretion of calcium. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Decrease the excretion of uric acid. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Are readily filtered thru the glomerulus. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Are entirely excreted thru kidneys. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"06b0d8a9420db17d074d65376baf42f61713f388"},{"id":"q-06284b54b578","stem":"Thiazide diuretic when used in the management of uncomplicated hypertension","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Cardiology","topicOriginal":"Cardiology","subtopic":"Hypertension","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduce the risk of stroke. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Are natriuretics. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Potassium supplements are usually required. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Reduce plasma rennin. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Are associated with impotence. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Cardiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"63dd54f3dc4dbdb2edbf4b93bb85525c53de4a12"},{"id":"q-45b63354f7a4","stem":"Organic nitrate like glyceryl nitrate | Antihypertensive","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduce preload. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Dilates collateral vessel which by pass narrowed coronary artery segment. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Have no effect on hemoglobin. F Methaemoglobin"},{"label":"D","text":"Dilates both veins and arterioles. T"},{"label":"E","text":"When used regularly become ineffective. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ffc387d3310e","stem":"The following compounds will cause both inhibition of platelets aggregation and vasodilation.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Hematology","topic":"Hematology","topicOriginal":"Hematology","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Epoprostenol T | PGI2"},{"label":"B","text":"Ticlopidine T"},{"label":"C","text":"Dipyridamole T"},{"label":"D","text":"Abciximab F"},{"label":"E","text":"Cilostazol T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Hematology","displayTopic":"Hematology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c9f3911380e","stem":"Severe Parenchyma liver disease is likely to increase the bioavailability of | hypert","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Propanolol T"},{"label":"B","text":"Lignocaine T"},{"label":"C","text":"Gylcerly trinitrate T"},{"label":"D","text":"Digoxin F"},{"label":"E","text":"Chloroquine F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f4b29e1c9766","stem":"The following drugs can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy in patients with several liver diseases.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Morphine T"},{"label":"B","text":"Aluminium hydroxide T"},{"label":"C","text":"Codeine T"},{"label":"D","text":"Propranolol T"},{"label":"E","text":"Stilboestrol T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b5a3f29377b787e745a3a694532f22cc2abe14e7"},{"id":"q-d23d01c2cee0","stem":"Simvastin; an HMGCoA reductase inhibitor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lowers low density lipoprotein cholesterol. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Is particulary useful in heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Acts locally on HMGCoA reductase in the intestine. F"},{"label":"D","text":"Is associated with rhabdomyolysis. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is ineffective if prescribed with bile acid binding resin. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"1a9687d5a84ad13df745942fc19f3a211cf7bed5"},{"id":"q-6a521857c449","stem":"HDL (High density lipoproteins)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has anti-atherogenic lipoprotein activity. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Retrieves cholesterol from artery wall. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Promotes oxidation of atherogenic lipoprotein. F"},{"label":"D","text":"In high levels is an independent risk factor for coronary disease. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Is metabolized to low density lipoprotein. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7f0b6af355e3e040850ea257e3ccf097451ff339"},{"id":"q-b3da4daa4b81","stem":"ACTH secretion is regulated by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Corticotrophin releasing factor derived from the hypothalamus. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Levels of thyrotrophic in the blood. F"},{"label":"C","text":"Anti diuretic hormone circulating in blood. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Level of hydrocortisone in the blood. T"},{"label":"E","text":"11-deoxycorticostrone. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"fe85e2e976889d782d079d380299aa6f02d76df7"},{"id":"q-35b4a5e9331b","stem":"Metyrapone inhibits","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"3-beta-dehydrogenase. F"},{"label":"B","text":"17-beta-hydroxylase. F"},{"label":"C","text":"11- beta-hydroxylase.T"},{"label":"D","text":"21- beta-hydroxylase. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Synthesis of corticosterone T."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"132e264c32086a570c96e895dd1b6f635df411a8"},{"id":"q-4f09dcb4b620","stem":"The following drugs may be useful in reducing symptoms of Cushing’s syndrome","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trilostane T"},{"label":"B","text":"Metyrapone T"},{"label":"C","text":"Ketokonazole T"},{"label":"D","text":"Aminoglutethimide T"},{"label":"E","text":"Prednisolone F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d8b2688bdbd80e539d4a31abc887d1bdca01c28e"},{"id":"q-5be18de67d0d","stem":"Asprin will antagonize the antihypertensive effect of bendrofluazide because asprin | thiazide diuretic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blocks receptors for bendrofluazide. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Increase the protein binding of bendrofluazide. F"},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces the natriuretic effect of the drug. T via prostaglandins"},{"label":"D","text":"Induces the liver to synthesize substances which bind the bendrofluazide. F"},{"label":"E","text":"Accelerates the metabolism of bendrofluazide. F"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e3067f04e421","stem":"Thiazide diuretics are associated with","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Diuretic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Purpura. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperuricaemia. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Hyperglycaemia. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Hypercalcaemia. T | inhibits Insulin secretion"},{"label":"E","text":"Hypercholesterolemia. T"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"4fea6f0e86502305d1247eb6d2e37776613d999a"},{"id":"q-74edd638d410","stem":"<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\" standalone=\"yes\"?>","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"<a:clrMap xmlns:a=\"http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/drawingml/2006/main\" bg1=\"lt1\" tx1=\"dk1\" bg2=\"lt2\" tx2=\"dk2\" accent1=\"accent1\" accent2=\"accent2\" accent3=\"accent3\" accent4=\"accent4\" accent5=\"accent5\" accent6=\"accent6\" hlink=\"hlink\" folHlink=\"folHlink\"/>"},{"label":"B","text":"*€urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:smarttags"},{"label":"C","text":"€stockticker"},{"label":"D","text":"ZYÂ!Xp"},{"label":"E","text":"}@Õ$Z@ ̧"},{"label":"F","text":"W#%Œ<þ6ÿ"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d345471c620f51033193346114ce04085734c807"},{"id":"q-cbe3a8a90379","stem":"A 56-year-old man was admitted to the hospital with low grade fever, night sweats, arthralgia an a 15 pounds weight loss. Past history was significant for rheumatic fever at the age of 9 and for dental surgery two months ago. The aforementioned symptoms started about two weeks after the dental procedure. Physical examination was significant for a diastolic murmur with mitral regurgitation, subungual splinter hemorrhages and hemorrhagic plaques on the soles of both feet. Two blood cultures showed gram-positive bacteria. Which of the following would be an appropriate drug treatment for this patient? The history and the clinical presentation of the patient strongly suggest that the man is suffering from infective endocarditis. He appears chronically ill and represents the typical patient with subacute disease. He has mitral valve prolapse which is the predominant defect in infective endocarditis and he also exhibits several peripheral manifestation of infective endocarditis including hemorrhages in the hands and feet. The gram-positive bacteria most commonly involved in infective endocarditis are streptococci and staphylococci. Streptococcus viridans is the principal cause of endocarditis in an abnormal heart valve (which is present in this case) and it often reaches the blood stream as a result of trauma. The temporal relationship between the dental procedure and the onset of symptoms make it the most obvious cause of bacteremia. Most strain of S. viridans are sensitive to penicillin G and the drug acts synergistically with an aminoglycoside.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalexin and erythromycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxicillin and clindamycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Aztreonam and amikacin"},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillin G and gentamicin"},{"label":"E","text":"Piperacillin and ceftriaxone A, B) These are combinations of a bactericidal plus a bacteriostatic antibiotic. In general these combinations should be avoided (with few exceptions) since an antagonistic interaction would occur in most cases."},{"label":"C","text":"These drugs are effective mainly against gram-negative bacteria."},{"label":"E","text":"The combinations of two beta-lactam antibiotics that have the same mechanism of action would be irrational."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-00b096d7e256","stem":"A 79-year-old woman resident of a nursing facility complained of burning on urination and bladder pain. Microscopic examination of the urine sediment revealed many gram-positive bacteria and a urine culture indicated Enterococcus faecalis as the sole causative agent. Which of the following beta lactam drugs would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefoxitin"},{"label":"B","text":"Cefepime"},{"label":"C","text":"Cephalexin"},{"label":"D","text":"Amoxicillin"},{"label":"E","text":"Aztreonam"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-880f58f514cc","stem":"A 59-year-old man developed an intra-abdominal infection after a surgical resection of a colonic carcinoma. Ascitic fluid and blood cultures indicated Bacteroides fragilis as the main causative agent and an appropriate antibiotic treatment was started. The most appropriate drug for this patient would be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"vancomycin."},{"label":"B","text":"bacitracin."},{"label":"C","text":"aztreonam."},{"label":"D","text":"cloxacillin."},{"label":"E","text":"ciprofloxacin."},{"label":"F","text":"cefoxitin."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9fb4b06fe4ea","stem":"A 21-year-old woman presented with urinary frequency, dysuria and a mucopurulent vaginal discharge. A Gram stain revealed gram-negative cocci. Past history of the patient was significant for a severe allergic reaction to penicillin. Which of the following drugs would be contraindicated in this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Doxycycline"},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"label":"D","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-da6b6c3a543b","stem":"A 73-year-old man was admitted to the hospital with severe abdominal pain, nausea and vomiting, and temperature of 102.2 F. A diagnosis of acute cholecystitis was made and the patient was scheduled for cholecystectomy. Which of the following antibiotics would be most appropriate as a prophylactic agent before surgery?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piperacillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Tetracycline"},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Aztreonam"},{"label":"E","text":"Cefazolin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-18293330cb68","stem":"Since surgical wound infections are major nosocomial infections, antimicrobial prophylaxis is advisable in case of contaminated and clean-contaminated surgical procedures, especially in high risk patients (age over 70, acute inflammation, in the present case). The goal of antimicrobial prophylaxis is to decrease bacterial counts below critical level necessary to cause infection. Cefazolin is the drug most frequently used for surgical prophylaxis.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"General Surgery","topic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Broad spectrum antibiotics should be avoided for prophylaxis since they can alter microbial flora and can increase the emergence of microbial resistance. B, C) Bacteriostatic agents are not used for microbial prophylaxis since bacteremia can occur when the drug is no longer present."},{"label":"D","text":"Aztreonam is active only against gram-negative bacteria and therefore is not suitable to prevent infection due to staphylococci, that are the predominant organisms in surgical infections."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"General Surgery","displayTopic":"Trauma and Emergency Surgery · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e79bdffd7ddf","stem":"A 55-year-old woman presented to the hospital complaining of 3 weeks of night sweats, cough productive of a foul-smelling sputum and about a 10 lb weight loss. Physical examination showed fever (100 F), clear lung fields and mild clubbing. Chest radiography showed a thick-walled abscess in the left lobe. The Gram stain of the sputum revealed gram-negative bacilli and an aerobic culture was negative. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampicillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"C","text":"Meropenem"},{"label":"D","text":"Aztreonam"},{"label":"E","text":"Cefotaxime"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-38c306239140","stem":"A 53-year-old man, who was hospitalized for bacterial endocarditis, has been receiving a treatment with two drugs given IV. One week into therapy, a routine urinalysis showed albuminuria, microscopic hematuria and hyaline casts. Which of the following pairs of drugs could have causes these finding ?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampicillin and cephalexin"},{"label":"B","text":"Amikacin and tobramycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin and tobramycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Vancomycin and gentamicin"},{"label":"E","text":"Aztreonam and erythromycin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5f39e75dc5c4","stem":"Treatment of bacterial endocarditis requires the use of one or, most often, two bactericidal antibiotics. A cell wall synthesis inhibitor plus an aminoglycoside is the treatment of choice, since a synergistic effect can be obtained. In the present case vancomycin plus gentamicin was the most likely treatment, since the patient showed sings of nephrotoxicity. The incidence of nephrotoxicity associated with vancomycin administration alone is very low, but it increases substantially (up to 20%) in patients receiving vancomycin plus an aminoglycoside. A, E) These combinations are not used for bacterial endocarditis and are not nephrotoxic.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Microbiology","topic":"Microorganisms and Infection","topicOriginal":"Microorganisms and Infection","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"This combination can cause nephrotoxicity but it is irrational to use together two antibiotics with the same mechanism of action"},{"label":"C","text":"This combination can cause nephrotoxicity (due to the aminoglycoside) but is not used for bacterial endocarditis (in general a combination of a bactericidal plus a bacteriostatic antibiotic should be avoided, since an antagonistic interaction would occur"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Microbiology","displayTopic":"Microorganisms and Infection","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2eb22556e18a","stem":"A 5-year-old boy with recurrent pulmonary infections is brought to the hospital by his mother because of respiratory difficulties. A Gram stain of the sputum revealed gram-positive cocci in grape-like clusters. Which of the following drugs would be the best empiric treatment at this early stage?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin G"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampicillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Piperacillin"},{"label":"D","text":"Oxacillin"},{"label":"E","text":"Amoxicillin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d645e48545cc","stem":"A 28-year-old man being treated with a systemic antibiotic for primary syphilis presents with sudden onset of fever, oliguria and a skin rash. Laboratory findings show eosinophiluria and proteinuria. A diagnosis is made of drug-induced tubulointerstitial nephritis. Which of the following antibiotics most likely caused the disease of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin G"},{"label":"B","text":"Cefepime"},{"label":"C","text":"Rifampin"},{"label":"D","text":"Methicillin"},{"label":"E","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ff06066ae919","stem":"A previously healthy 3-year-old child was admitted to the hospital with a sore throat, fever, chills, painful swallowing, and dyspnea of 10-hour duration. Physical examination revealed edematous erythema over the anterior neck, cervical lymph node enlargement, crackles at left base and diffuse wheezing, best heard as stridor over neck. A sputum Gram stain showed numerous gram-negative coccobacilli consistent with Haemophilus influenzae. A diagnosis of acute supraglottitis was made and an appropriate therapy was immediately started. Which of the following drug was most likely given IV to the patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxacillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin G"},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"label":"D","text":"Vancomycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Cefazolin Protein Synthesis inhibitors"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4016376d34b5","stem":"An 1160 gm male, preterm born to a febrile mother, presented with respiratory distress and very low Apgar scores. Physical examination and lab results suggested neonatal sepsis and an empirical antibiotic therapy was instituted. Later, blood cultures indicated Listeria monocytogenes as the offending organism. Which of the following pairs of antibiotics would be an appropriate treatment for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Metronidazole and tobramycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampicillin and gentamicin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ceftriaxone and tobramycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Aztreonam and tobramycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Piperacillin and cefotaxime"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-808615fcad6b","stem":"A 65-year old woman, who was complaining of fever and sore throat, was prescribed an antibiotic treatment. The patient was suffering from depression and has been on an antidepressant medication (amitriptyline) for six months. Two days later, she was admitted to the hospital with sustained ventricular tachycardia. Which of the following antibiotics could have caused this adverse effect?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Vancomycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"label":"E","text":"Ampicillin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-67505d227e21","stem":"A 6-year-old boy presented with low grade fever, sore throat, nonproductive cough and headache. Physical examination revealed a maculopapular rash over the trunk and chest x-ray showed extensive nodular infiltrates in the left lower lobe. The presumptive diagnosis of mycoplasma pneumonia was confirmed later by the complement fixation test. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this boy?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tetracycline"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampicillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Cefotaxime"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptomycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Metronidazole"},{"label":"F","text":"Erythromycin"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8e0d8479c6e7","stem":"A 4-year-old boy was admitted to the pediatric ward with an acute tracheobronchitis characterized by sneezing, lacrimation and a paroxysmal, hacking cough. Several consecutive forceful coughs occurred during a single expiration and were followed by a hurried deep inspiration. The cough was accompanied by production of copious amount of mucus. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this child?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Aztreonam"},{"label":"B","text":"Cefotaxime"},{"label":"C","text":"Piperacillin"},{"label":"D","text":"Amikacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Erythromycin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-001f8a4d3a11","stem":"A 62-year-old alcoholic man presented to the Emergency Room with a fever (103 F), chest pain and cough productive of mucoid sputum. Physical examination revealed a man in obvious distress with the following vital sings: blood pressure 140/85 mm Hg, pulse 55 bpm, respirations 22/min. Sputum culture showed gram-negative bacilli able to grow only on a charcoal yeast extract agar consistent with a Legionella infection. Which of the following antibiotics would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin G"},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Vancomycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"F","text":"Metronidazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4947cded216c","stem":"A 45-year-old woman, who was on vacation in the south of Italy, presented with fever, headache and a small, button like skin ulcer, 5 mm in diameter, with a black center. The diagnosis of mediterranean spotted fever was made and the woman was prescribed doxycycline. Which of the following statements best explains the most likely mechanism of action of this drug?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibition of DNA-dependent RNA polymerase"},{"label":"B","text":"Blockade of the formation of ribosomal initiation complex"},{"label":"C","text":"Blockade of peptide bond formation"},{"label":"D","text":"Blockade of binding of aminoacyl-tRNA to bacterial ribosomes"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of transpeptidase enzymes"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c7a613bbdac3","stem":"Mediterranean spotted fever (also called fievre boutonneuse) is a disease caused by Rickettsia conorii. Doxycycline is a tetracycline antibiotic that has became the drug of choice for most rickettsial diseases, because of its efficacy, long half-life and very good oral bioavailability. Tetracyclines bind reversibly to the 30s ribosomal subunit and block the access of aminoacyl-tRNA to the acceptor side of the mRNA ribosome complex. This prevents the addition of amino acids to the growing peptide chain and therefore inhibits bacterial protein synthesis","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of rifampin"},{"label":"B","text":"This would be one of the mechanisms of action of aminoglycosides."},{"label":"C","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of chloramphenicol"},{"label":"E","text":"This would be the mechanism of action of beta lactam antibiotics"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8c75b0332253","stem":"A 16-year-old girl presented with a 5-day history of vulvar itching and burning and a thick, cheesy vaginal discharge. The girl has been taking an oral antibiotic for two months for her acne. A Gram stain of vaginal smear indicated Candida albicans as the offending pathogen. Which of the following antibiotics was she most likely taking?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefotaxime"},{"label":"B","text":"Amikacin"},{"label":"C","text":"Aztreonam"},{"label":"D","text":"Fluconazole"},{"label":"E","text":"Tetracycline"},{"label":"F","text":"Amphotericin B"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe3c34e9a63c","stem":"A 45-year-old man presented to the hospital with fever (103 F ), severe retrobulbar headache, chills, photophobia, muscular pains and prostration. He reported he was bitten by a tick, found attached to his scalp, 7 days ago while camping in the mountains in the western United States. Physical examination revealed a rash of pink macules 2 to 5 mm, spread all over his body. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Imipenem"},{"label":"B","text":"Doxycycline"},{"label":"C","text":"Piperacillin"},{"label":"D","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"E","text":"Cefotaxime"},{"label":"F","text":"Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-af525607aeee","stem":"A 71-year-old woman resident of a nursing facility was admitted to the hospital because of high fever, shortness of breath and cough productive of purulent sputum. A chest X-ray showed several areas of infiltration with possible abscess formation. A blood culture indicated that the infection was due to staphylococci and an antibiotic sensitivity test showed that they were resistant to methicillin and vancomycin. Which of the following antibiotics would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nafcillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Tetracycline"},{"label":"C","text":"Amikacin"},{"label":"D","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"label":"E","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"F","text":"Quinupristin/dalfopristin"}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0319dfbb9012","stem":"A 52-year-old black man suffering from chronic urinary tract infection presented to the hospital complaining of low grade fever, urinary frequency and burning sensation upon urination. Past history was significant for glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency. He started the treatment prescribed by his urologist but five days later he began feeling tired and noticed his urine had become dark. Urinalysis revealed bilirubin and urobilinogen. Which of the following drugs could have caused these findings?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Piperacillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Gentamicin"},{"label":"C","text":"Quinupristin/dalfopristin"},{"label":"D","text":"Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole"},{"label":"E","text":"Ciprofloxacin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-11a4bc6ecd98","stem":"A 22-year-old man was admitted to the hospital because of an erythematous, macular eruption on the extremities, followed by the appearance of vesicles and bullae on the lips, oral mucosa, anogenital region and conjunctiva. A few days ago he started a treatment with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole for an acute pharyngitis. Vital signs on admission were: temperature 103 F, pulse 120 bpm, blood pressure 120/78 mm Hg, respirations 30/min. Which of the following statements best explains the syndrome of this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lupoid syndrome due to trimethoprim"},{"label":"B","text":"Lupoid syndrome due to sulfamethoxazole"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug-induced anaphylactic reaction"},{"label":"D","text":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome due to trimethoprim"},{"label":"E","text":"Stevens-Johnson syndrome due to sulfamethoxazole"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4cedd9455ca5","stem":"A 30-year-old, HIV seropositive man presents with nonproductive cough, moderate fever, mild dyspnea, facial seborrheic dermatitis and a CD4 lymphocyte count of 150/mm3. A silver-stained preparation from bronchial lavage revealed a large number of cysts containing sporozoites. Which of the following drugs would be most appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole"},{"label":"B","text":"Aztreonam"},{"label":"C","text":"Penicillin G"},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Gentamicin"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6bbf76533490","stem":"A 35-year-old woman was admitted to the hospital with one month history of fever, anorexia and weight loss. Physical examination revealed a systolic heart murmur, splinter hemorrhages and petechiae on her soft palate. The patient had a documented allergy to penicillins. The presumptive diagnosis of bacterial endocarditis was confirmed when four sets of blood cultures identified Enterococcus faecalis as the offending pathogen. The Enterococcus was found to be highly resistant to aminoglycosides and to vancomycin. Which of the following drug would be appropriate for this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampicillin"},{"label":"B","text":"Cefepime"},{"label":"C","text":"Daptomycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Azithromycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Doxycycline"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-27d5b9ecff72","stem":"The standard therapy for endocarditis caused by Enterococcus faecalis is penicillin G (or ampicillin) plus gentamicin (or another aminoglycoside). In case of allergy to penicillins, vancomycin is the standard substitute. In this patient however the enterococcus is resistant to both aminoglycosides and vancomycin. Daptomycin, which depolarizes the cell membrane, inhibits DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis. It is active against enterococci (including vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus faecium and Enterococcus faecalis), as well as against MR and vancomycin resistant staphylococci.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ampicillin cannot be used, because of the allergy to penicillins."},{"label":"B","text":"Enterococci are resistant to all cephalosporins (remember LAME). D, E) Macrolides and tetracyclines are not active against enterococci."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2459c19eef1d","stem":"trogen receptor modulators) would be appropriate to give to decrease the likelihood of further tumors developing? Tamoxifen Raloxifene Toremifene Clomiphene Tamoxifen and toremifene combination. A patient has become infertile because a prolactinoma has developed in her anterior pituitary gland. Instead of surgical intervention, pharmacological intervention for treating this tumor includes which of the following drugs? Bromocriptine Pergolide Quinoglide Cabergline Pramipexole. A patient with hyperthyroidism is scheduled for thyroid gland surgery. In order to reduce the size and vascularity of the thyroid gland, making it technically easier to excise, which of the following drugs should be given before surgery? Thiocyanate, perchlorate, or pertechnetate 131Iodide Stable inorganic iodide Propylthiouracil Methimazole. After an immunocompromised patient undergoes surgery, which of the following antibiotics would be appropriate based on their bacteriostatic/bacteriocidal activities? Rifampin Aminoglycosides Mac","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c766a7c5dfa","stem":"Postural hypotension is a common adverse effect of which one of the following types of drugs?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c650ecbf7cc91cce01e1dcfe301731758e9c445"},{"id":"q-22f6b5c2e893","stem":"W